+// Body-condition matcher for the legacy policy rules.
+//
+// A rule's `conditions` column holds a JSON array of conditions, and the rule applies to a request
+// only when every condition holds. We support one target and two operators, because the coworker
+// deployment uses them to keep closed models away from the open agent:
+//
+//   {"target": "body", "key": "model", "operator": "not_in", "value": "nvidia/*,openai/nvidia/*"}
+//
+// `target = "body"` reads the JSON request body and `key` is a dotted path into it. The value is
+// a comma-separated list of globs (a JSON array of strings also works), where `*` matches any run
+// of characters, because model ids contain `/`. The web API keeps `value` a string, so a list
+// travels as one comma-separated string. `in` holds when
+// the field is present and matches a glob. `not_in` holds when the field is missing, is not a
+// string, or matches no glob, so a block rule with `not_in` fails closed: a request whose model we
+// cannot read is refused rather than let through.
+// A body whose top-level object repeats the first segment of `key`, in any ASCII case, counts as
+// unreadable, because another JSON parser on the way could read a different copy.
+//
+// `target = "query"` reads a parameter of the URL's query string, and `key` is its name. Names match in
+// any ASCII case and both the percent-decoded forms count, because the server behind the gateway may
+// decode once or twice. A query parameter is optional, unlike the model of a request body, so the
+// condition holds only when the parameter is there: `not_in` holds when some copy of it matches no
+// glob, and `in` when every copy matches one. A WebSocket handshake carries its model this way
+// (`/v1/realtime?model=...`), and the gateway decides it as a GET with no body.
+//
+// A condition we do not understand (another target, another operator, a malformed value) holds, as
+// every condition did before this matcher existed. The decision cannot depend on the rule's action,
+// because the v2 engine matches every rule through a throwaway rule whose action is always `Allow`.
+// A rule that must not be skipped therefore uses `not_in` on a block rule, which holds when the
+// field is unreadable, and an allow rule that must not widen uses `in`.
+
+/// The largest request body we buffer to read a condition. A larger body, or one of unknown length,
+/// streams through unread, and the body conditions then see no body.
+const MAX_BUFFERED_BODY: usize = 32 * 1024 * 1024;
+
+/// A request body that carries JSON-sized data: the methods that can have a body.
+fn method_has_body(method: &str) -> bool {
+    matches!(
+        method.to_ascii_uppercase().as_str(),
+        "POST" | "PUT" | "PATCH"
+    )
+}
